{
  "case_id": "2021-20-603",
  "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew T. Tutt",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. TUTT: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The Constitution gave Congress the power to raise and support Armies, and the reason for that grant was to ensure the survival of the nation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, what do you do about our decision in Allen, which seemed to suggest that Katz, on which you rely, was quite specific and limited to that context?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I don't quite understand the distinction that you're making between those two things. Could you -- could you explain that, why you think that these are in two separate buckets?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Texas (via Mr. Tutt)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- the states do not participate in raising and supporting the Army. That is an exclusively federal power."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christopher G. Michel (United States)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice, and may it please the Court: Raising and supporting military forces is among the United States' express constitutional powers and most essential responsibilities. USERRA directly advances that mission. Its employment protections originated with the World War II draft. They were extended to permit suits against states to combat discrimination against the military during the Vietnam War. And they are especially important today to Guard and Reserve forces, who both serve the nation and work for employers, disproportionately including state employers. Those employers have sovereign immunity to most private suits, but this area is different."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I guess I would have thought that the abrogation cases are also in part not only about whether Congress has spoken clearly but whether, even if Congress did speak clearly, its word would govern. Isn't that what they're about? And -- and, in order to answer that question, aren't we looking at the same kinds of things that we're looking at to determine whether there's an exception under the Plan of the Convention?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "there's an amicus brief that has statistics about the number of cases that the Justice Department has brought. It says that in the 16 years from 2004 to 2020, the court -- the Justice Department filed 109 lawsuits, which is a little more than six a year, and that only two were filed from 2015 -- since 2015, only two have been filed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm just wondering what the limits are of the principle you're asking us to adopt. I understand the textual commitments in the Fourteenth Amendment, but, here, we're being asked to adopt a view of implicit penumbras emanating from the War Powers Act -- sorry, from the war powers that the president and the Congress have in Article I and Article II."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Michel)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This -- I think there is an argument in this case that allowing these lawsuits is necessary to raising and supporting Armies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You're not arguing, though, that we -- we have other -- we don't have other mechanisms to raise and support Armies?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Michel",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think it lies at the -- at least in this case, at the perimeter of the Raise and Support Armies Clause. I don't think that just because Congress or some litigant asserts that something is within the Raise and Support Armies Clause --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And just on the -- you -- you alluded to this, but why is it necessary today to have this kind of law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus brief (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0003-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In fact, as one of the amicus briefs in this case points out, the brochure that the Army gives to its recruits lists the USERRA protections as part of the incentive package that they receive to join the military."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stone",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Torres's contrary argument rests on two premises: first, that the Constitution delegates a plenary and exclusive war power to Congress and, second, that the erection of state sovereign immunity impermissibly frustrates the exercise of those war powers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Congress",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress has specifically given them a cause of action against the states under USERRA to pursue remedies in -- in federal court against aggrieved servicemembers"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0004-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's a consequence of immunity in any context."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 105,
        "start": 105
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Stone, could -- General Stone, could you comment on how far you think the argument would go if we agree with Petitioners?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew T. Tutt (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f97cb93343c60212f71899ca015159a7b2d6d50d383c03155c3c66371643137",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f97cb93343c-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 106,
        "start": 106
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. TUTT: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Just a few points. Texas opened by saying that it's a means/ends distinction, that that's what's at stake, that the powers may be great, but the means can be limited. But, if you go to the Federalist Number 23 by Alexander Hamilton, he addresses this directly, and he says that the means ought to be proportioned to the end."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-20-603",
      "confidence": 0.0,
